'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To put into a state of ebullition; cause to be agitated or to bubble by the application of heat. Hence—'''2.''' To collect, form, or separate by the application of heat, as sugar, salt, etc.—'''3.''' To subject to the action of heat in a liquid raised to its point of ebullition, so as to produce some specific effect; cook or seethe in a boiling liquid: as, to ''boil'' meat, potatoes, etc.; to ''boil'' silk, thread, etc.—{{smaller|'''To boil clear''', in ''soap-manuf.'', to remove the excess of water from soft soap by boiling it. A concentrated lye is employed to shorten the time of evaporation.—'''To boil down''', to reduce in bulk by boiling; hence, to reduce to smaller compass by removal of what can best be spared; condense by elimination.}}
{{smaller block|After a while he [Bowles] developed a talent for condensing into brief and readable form the long and heavy articles in which the great political papers of the day discharged their thunder. On these he began to practice that great art of ''boiling down'' which his paper afterward carried to such perfection. ''G. S. Merriam'', S. Bowles, I. 23.}}
{{smaller|'''To boil dry''', in ''sugar-manuf.'', to reduce the thin juice to thick juice by boiling it until it reaches the point of crystallization.}}

